 id=\"text_15\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(321.382486 14.928) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-84\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-85\" transform=\"translate(64.90625 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(130.3125 0)\"/></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"p9dda3e6ac9\"><rect x=\"26.233281\" y=\"10.296\" width=\"345.96\" height=\"138.6\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 2 · US premium e-bike share of units, %","note":null},"answer":"<p>The market is <b>fragmented</b>: the top four hold 55% and a long tail holds 45%. A European brand (B) already holds 15%, so US buyers clearly accept European premium bikes. To reach 8%, Nordwind needs to match Brand D. <b>So-what:</b> the share target is realistic if Nordwind gets its bikes into enough shops. That makes the route to market the key decision.</p>"},{"q":"Nordwind is choosing between two ways to enter. Which is better?","exhibit":{"kind":"table","headers":["","A · US distributor","B · Own sales network"],"rows":[["Upfront investment","None","$12M (team, warehouse, launch)"],["Annual fixed cost","$1.5M (marketing)","$6M"],["Distributor fee","15% of price to shops","—"],["Variable cost per bike (make, ship, duty)","$1,500","$1,500"]],"title":"Exhibit 3 · Entry options","note":null,"align":["l","r","r"],"boldLast":false},"answer":"<p><b>Contribution per bike:</b> A = $2,400 × 85% − $1,500 = <b>$540</b>; B = $2,400 − $1,500 = <b>$900</b>.</p> <p><b>Annual profit is the same when</b> 540V − 1.5M = 900V − 6M → 360V = 4.5M → <b>V = 12,500 bikes/year</b>.</p> <p>Below 12,500 bikes a year the distributor is better; above it, the own network wins. At year-5 volume (~18,300): A ≈ $8.4M, B ≈ $10.5M.</p> <p>Own-network breakeven = $6M ÷ $900 ≈ 6,700 bikes/year. If volumes ramp 3k → 7k → 11k → 15k → 18.3k, B makes a small profit in year 2, but recovering the $12M upfront takes until year 5.</p>","note":"Let the candidate set up the indifference equation. Good candidates realise the answer is 'distributor first, then switch'."},{"q":"What risks should the founders weigh?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li><b>Tariffs and trade policy</b> on EU-made goods can wipe out the $900 margin overnight. Consider US final assembly.</li> <li><b>Distributor lock-in</b>: a 5-year exclusive contract would block the switch to an own network.</li> <li><b>Brand awareness</b>: no US brand recognition, so early volumes may be slower.</li> <li><b>Service</b>: e-bikes need repairs and spare parts; shops need training.</li></ul>"}],"reco":"Yes, enter the US, but through a distributor first. Hitting $44M needs about 8% of the premium segment, which is achievable in a fragmented market. A distributor is more profitable until volumes pass ~12,500 bikes a year and needs no $12M upfront, so it also meets the 'profitable by year 3' goal more safely. The contract should be 3 years or less, with the right to take over distribution once that volume is reached. Main risk: tariffs. As a next step, model US assembly and start in 2-3 bike-friendly states.","great":["States the 8% share and judges whether it's realistic","Sets up the 12,500-bike crossover clearly","Recommends a sequence (distributor → own network), not just one option","Raises tariffs without prompting"],"id":2,"slug":"nordwind-e-bikes"}